ip", "Prediction markets price Mendoza near 90 percent to win", "Mendoza leads fundraising $10.8M to $7.6M with a wide cash edge and heavy outside spending against Ciscomani", "Even-PVI district in a Democratic-favorable midterm environment; the only polls are old but all show Mendoza narrowly ahead"], "rationale": "A favorable midterm environment, Mendoza's clear money advantage, and near-unanimous expert ratings calling for a flip point to a Democratic gain, and markets price her around 90 percent. The available polls are months old and show only narrow leads, so uncertainty remains elevated.", "watch": "Fresh polling showing Ciscomani closing the gap, or a late surge of Republican-aligned outside spending, could pull the race back toward a true tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Juan Ciscomani (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Arizona's 6th District sits at even partisan balance, and Republican incumbent Juan Ciscomani is seeking another term against Democrat JoAnna Mendoza, a veteran of two decades in the Navy and Marines. Expert raters nearly uniformly shift the seat toward Democrats this fall: nine of ten call it a Democratic lean or better, most projecting a flip, and only one rates it a tossup. Prediction markets similarly price Mendoza as a strong favorite, near 90 percent.\n\nThe money race favors the challenger. Mendoza has raised about $10.8 million to Ciscomani's $7.6 million and holds a wide cash-on-hand advantage, while outside groups have spent heavily against the incumbent. Public polling is sparse and dated, but each available survey shows Mendoza narrowly ahead. A favorable midterm environment for Democrats underpins the outlook, though late spending and Latino voter turnout could still tighten the contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8163, "margin": 5.47}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8157, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "